<VI _name="RemoteVI Translate Input.vi" description="Turns one parsed client input message (the outputs of RemoteVI Parse Input.vi) into the Win32 messages the server posts to the panel window (docs/protocol.md section 4). Only the allowed messages of section 4.3 produce posts; anything else - ack\2C invalid\2C WM_CLOSE\2C WM_SYSKEYDOWN - gives an empty posts array and leaves tracking unchanged. WM_CHAR becomes three posts: WM_KEYDOWN(0xE8\2C1)\2C WM_CHAR(wParam\2C1)\2C WM_KEYUP(0xE8\2C0xC0000001). Other key messages pass through\2C except that a WM_KEYDOWN/WM_KEYUP whose lParam has no scan code (bits 16-23 zero - the browser client sends none) gets the key's set-1 scan code from the table below (LabVIEW lost the arrow key of a posted Shift + Right when both had scan code 0\2C measured). A double click (WM_xBUTTONDBLCLK) is posted as a second WM_xBUTTONDOWN\3A LabVIEW's panel window class has no CS_DBLCLKS\2C so Windows sends it two downs and LabVIEW ignores a posted DBLCLK (measured with a listbox Double Click event). Mouse lParam = ((y and 0xFFFF) shl 16) or (x and 0xFFFF) of CLIENT coordinates (screen point minus client origin) or of SCREEN coordinates (panel origin plus x/y): the wheel always uses screen; a button-down uses client and starts tracking; a button-up uses screen while tracking in is TRUE\2C else client\2C and ends tracking; a move uses screen while tracking in is TRUE\2C else client. The server passes as tracking in whether LabVIEW tracks the press right now - its UI thread holds the mouse capture (RemoteVI Mouse Captured.vi)\2C read after LabVIEW handled the button-down. LabVIEW reads moves and the button-up as SCREEN coordinates only while it tracks (latched buttons\2C sliders\2C text selection\2C graph drags)\3B a switch-when-pressed does not track and lost a screen-coordinate button-up (measured). While a LabVIEW popup is open (popup TRUE) only BUTTON messages are posted\3A with SCREEN coordinates\2C and cursor asks the caller to move the host cursor to that screen point just for the click (popup menus read the real cursor)\3B tracking follows the same button rules. Moves and the wheel are DROPPED while a popup is open (no posts\2C tracking unchanged\2C no cursor)\2C so the host cursor stays where the host user left it. Key messages are unaffected by popup. Pure function\2C cannot fail. Reference: translateInput() in tools\5Clib\5Cinput.mjs.">
